At the Tuesday night (July 28) performance at Bell Centre in the Canadian province, Grande expanded her usual setlist to include a performance of &#8220;Petal,&#8221; the title track of her eighth studio album arriving at the end of the week. Wearing the white dress she sports toward the end of her set &#8212; which concludes with her being pulled up to the sky by a UFO &#8212; the vocalist premieres the song&#8217;s slinking verses and ascending, atmospheric chorus.

&#8220;All of my favorite stories end in some kind of catastrophe/ But I don&#8217;t need someone to save me/ ’Cause this music and I will never die,&#8221; she sings over harmonies and her drummer&#8217;s pounding percussion. &#8220;Tired of printing endless pictures of a life that isn’t mine/ Trying different boys on for size/ If this is my curse, I’ll let it rise.&#8221;

Elsewhere in Tuesday&#8217;s show, Grande sang part of &#8220;Worst Behavior&#8221; from the deluxe edition of her 2021 Positions album for the first time live.

The next day, Grande shared Instagram photos from the set of her upcoming visual project &#8212; likely a music video, the 1950s-esque teaser for which she dropped earlier Tuesday &#8212; and captioned it with more &#8220;Petal&#8221; lyrics. &#8220;just as long as she keeps getting it right heartbreak won’t bite,&#8221; she wrote. &#8220;this is paradise.&#8221;

In the teaser, the Wicked actress debuted a new character accompanying her Petal storytelling universe. &#8220;Hello, I&#8217;m Pepper,&#8221; she tells a receptionist in the black-and-white clip. &#8220;I&#8217;m here for the audition.&#8221;

Petal is set to drop Friday (July 31), two years after the release of Eternal Sunshine , which spent three weeks atop the Billboard 200 and produced two Billboard Hot 100 No. 1 hits: &#8220;Yes, And?&#8221; and &#8220;We Can&#8217;t Be Friends (Wait for Your Love).&#8221; The lead single of Grande&#8217;s next album, &#8220;Hate That I Made You Love Me,&#8221; debuted at the top of the Hot 100, marking her 10th career No. 1 hit.

The Grammy winner sings many of those chart smashes every night on the Eternal Sunshine Tour, which kicked off in early June. After two more nights in Montreal, she&#8217;ll move on to Chicago for three shows followed by a tour-closing 10-night stay at the O2 Arena in London.
